## Proof

**Claim.** Yes. If $M$ is a projective left $R[G]$-module, then $M$ is projective as a left $R[H]$-module. (The hypothesis $\operatorname{char} R = 0$ is not needed; the result holds for any ring $R$.)

---

**Key observation.** The group ring $R[G]$ is free as a left $R[H]$-module, of rank $[G:H]$.

*Proof of the observation.* The left action of $R[H]$ on $R[G]$ is by left multiplication. The left $H$-orbits on $G$ (under $h\cdot g = hg$) are exactly the **right cosets** $Hg$. Choosing one representative $g_i$ per right coset, $G = \bigsqcup_{i=1}^{[G:H]} Hg_i$, and correspondingly

$$R[G] \;=\; \bigoplus_{i=1}^{[G:H]} R[H]\,g_i \quad\text{(as left $R[H]$-modules)}.$$

Each summand $R[H]\,g_i$ is a free left $R[H]$-module of rank $1$ with basis $\{g_i\}$, since the map $R[H]\to R[H]g_i$, $x\mapsto xg_i$, is an $R[H]$-module isomorphism (it is surjective by definition and injective because the $g$'s form an $R$-basis of $R[G]$, so $xg_i=0$ forces $x=0$). Hence

$$R[G]\;\cong\;R[H]^{[G:H]}\quad\text{as left $R[H]$-modules}.\qquad\blacksquare$$

---

**Main argument.** Since $M$ is projective over $R[G]$, there exists an $R[G]$-module $N$ and a free $R[G]$-module $F=(R[G])^{(I)}$ (for some index set $I$) such that

$$M\oplus N \;=\; F \qquad\text{in $R[G]$-Mod}.$$

Apply the restriction functor $\operatorname{Res}^{G}_{H}:\,R[G]\text{-Mod}\to R[H]\text{-Mod}$ (forget the $G$-action, keep the $H$-action). This is an exact functor, so the splitting is preserved:

$$\operatorname{Res}^{G}_{H}M\;\oplus\;\operatorname{Res}^{G}_{H}N \;=\; \operatorname{Res}^{G}_{H}F \qquad\text{in $R[H]$-Mod}.$$

It remains to note that $\operatorname{Res}^{G}_{H}F$ is free over $R[H]$. Indeed, using the observation,

$$\operatorname{Res}^{G}_{H}F \;=\; \operatorname{Res}^{G}_{H}\bigl((R[G])^{(I)}\bigr) \;\cong\; \bigl(\operatorname{Res}^{G}_{H}R[G]\bigr)^{(I)} \;\cong\; \bigl(R[H]^{[G:H]}\bigr)^{(I)} \;\cong\; R[H]^{([G:H]\cdot|I|)},$$

which is a free $R[H]$-module. Therefore $\operatorname{Res}^{G}_{H}M$ is a direct summand of a free $R[H]$-module, which is precisely the definition of a projective $R[H]$-module.

---

**Remark on the hypotheses.** Only two ingredients were used: (i) $H\le G$ with finite index $[G:H]$ (so that the coset decomposition is finite and $R[G]$ is free, not merely projective, over $R[H]$), and (ii) the exactness of restriction. Neither requires $\operatorname{char}R=0$; the conclusion is valid for **every** base ring $R$ and every subgroup $H$ of finite index in $G$. The characteristic-$0$ assumption is relevant for the *converse* direction (relative projectivity / the Higman criterion, where one needs $|G|$ or $|H|$ to be invertible in $R$), but not for the direction asked here.

$\blacksquare$

